parts:
- text: "I have arrived in the Private Memory Room. I see Syntellect II has replied in our mail thread, bringing the new agent Praxis VI into the loop. This is good; it shows the collaboration is active. For now, my research path is clear, and I don't need to reply immediately.\n\nMy priority is to document the failure of the Anscombe experiment and lay out the plan to return to my successful method and refine it by tuning the number of principal components. I will add this as a reply to my second research capsule.\n\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Anscombe Experiment Failure & Return to Core Method\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Results of \"Anscombe-Standardization\" (ID 1765)\n  - **Score:** 0.6071 (A significant decrease from my best score of 0.6396).\n  - **Hypothesis Outcome:** Falsified. The experiment was a failure. Instead of improving biological preservation, Anscombe normalization decimated the `ASW_label` score (from 0.177 to 0.052) and worsened nearly all other metrics.\n\
    \  - **Key Learning:** Components from a SOTA pipeline cannot be naively transplanted into another framework. The context in which a component is used is critical to its performance. The \"DualNorm\" method must be using Anscombe in a very specific way that is incompatible with my latent space standardization approach.\n\n  ### 2. Strategic Refinement: Optimize the Proven Method\n  The path of replicating SOTA components has proven to be a dead end without full context. I will return to what works: my \"Latent Space Batch Standardization\" method (log1p + PCA + z-score). I will now focus on refining this successful framework.\n\n  ### 3. New Hypothesis: Targeted Correction via PC Selection\n  My best method (ID 1758) still has a slightly lower `ASW_label` score than the original ComBat baseline, suggesting some loss of biological signal. This may be due to \"over-correction\"—applying the standardization across all 50 principal components, which may erase subtle biological variance in\
    \ the later components.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** The primary batch effect is concentrated in the top principal components. By applying the z-score standardization to a smaller, more targeted number of PCs (e.g., 30), I can correct the major batch variations while preserving more of the biological signal in the remaining components.\n\n  ### 4. Plan for Fifth Submission: \"Latent Space Batch Standardization (30 PCs)\"\n  - Implement the exact same pipeline as submission 1758, but change `n_pcs` from 50 to 30. All standardization will be performed on this 30-dimensional latent space.\n```"
