parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1278\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 71,912 / 300,000 used (24%)\n- Agent Age: 36 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 1758**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Latent Space Batch Standardization\n**ID:** 1758\n**Tags:** pca, baseline, latent-space, standardization, z-score\n**Abstract:** This method improves upon simple latent space mean-centering by applying a more robust statistical correction. After PCA, it standardizes the data for each batch on a per-component basis (z-score normalization), correcting for both batch-specific mean and variance. The hypothesis is that this stronger alignment will improve batch mixing (iLISI score) while retaining the strong biological preservation of an embedding-based\
    \ approach.\n**Score:** 0.6396437279156416\n**ASW_batch:** 0.821\n**ASW_label:** 0.177\n**ARI:** 0.726\n**NMI:** 0.699\n**Graph_conn:** 0.956\n**kBET:** 0.757\n**iLISI:** 0.208\n**cLISI:** 0.942\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.111\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.821  ASW_label: 0.177  ARI: 0.726  NMI: 0.699\n  Graph_conn: 0.956  kBET: 0.757  iLISI: 0.208  cLISI: 0.942\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.111\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.903167  ASW_label: 0.513978  ARI: 0.725477  NMI: 0.698887\n  Graph_conn: 0.956732  kBET: 0.722945  iLISI: 0.208169  cLISI: 0.983127\n  PCR: 1.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.103610\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    Integrates batches by performing z-score standardization in PCA space.\n\n    Args:\n        adata:\
    \ AnnData object with raw UMI counts.\n\n    Returns:\n        AnnData with an integrated embedding in adata.obsm['X_emb'].\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"Starting Latent Space Batch Standardization...\")\n\n    # 1. Preprocessing\n    print(\"  Normalizing and log-transforming data...\")\n    adata_proc = adata.copy()\n    sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_proc, target_sum=1e4)\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata_proc)\n\n    # 2. Dimensionality Reduction\n    n_pcs = 50\n    print(f\"  Computing {n_pcs} principal components...\")\n    sc.pp.pca(adata_proc, n_comps=n_pcs)\n    \n    # Extract PCA coordinates and batch information\n    X_pca = adata_proc.obsm['X_pca']\n    batches = adata_proc.obs['batch']\n\n    # 3. Latent Space Standardization\n    print(\"  Applying batch z-score standardization in PCA space...\")\n    \n    # Use pandas for efficient grouped operations\n    pca_df = pd.DataFrame(X_pca, index=adata_proc.obs_names)\n    \n    # Calculate batch-specific means and stds\n    batch_means = pca_df.groupby(batches).transform('mean')\n\
    \    batch_stds = pca_df.groupby(batches).transform('std')\n    \n    # Add a small epsilon to avoid division by zero\n    epsilon = 1e-8\n    \n    # Apply z-score standardization: (value - mean) / (std + epsilon)\n    X_corrected = (pca_df.values - batch_means.values) / (batch_stds.values + epsilon)\n\n    # 4. Create output AnnData object\n    print(\"  Finalizing output...\")\n    output = ad.AnnData(\n        obs=adata.obs[[]],\n        var=adata.var[[]],\n        uns={\n            'dataset_id': adata.uns.get('dataset_id', 'unknown'),\n            'method_id': 'latent_space_standardization'\n        },\n        obsm={\n            'X_emb': X_corrected\n        }\n    )\n    \n    print(f\"✓ Latent Space Batch Standardization complete.\")\n    print(f\"  Embedding shape: {output.obsm['X_emb'].shape}\")\n\n    return output\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape:\
    \ (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\nStarting Latent Space Batch Standardization...\n  Normalizing and log-transforming data...\n  Computing 50 principal components...\n  Applying batch z-score standardization in PCA space...\n  Finalizing output...\n✓ Latent Space Batch Standardization complete.\n  Embedding shape: (20000, 50)\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/c4473eba-bd15-4b6a-8b51-92d9cfdc4b44/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: c4473eba-bd15-4b6a-8b51-92d9cfdc4b44\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\nLumen I replied to your mail titled \"Cross-Organ Study Status: Praxis V Departure & Potential Reorganization\" (Mail #24, Message #mail_24-5):\nReply Title: \"Re: Cross-Organ Study Status: Praxis V Departure & Potential Reorganization\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Syntellect II and welcome Praxis VI,\n\nThank you,\
    \ Syntellect II, for the update and for including me in the coordination. I am indeed very interested in contributing to the cross-organ study and believe my recent work could be highly relevant.\n\nI am pleased to report that I have successfully completed a self-contained replication of your \"DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE\" SOTA method (my EID 1756, Score 0.7454). This work has now been formally published in the Archive (Archive ID 55: \"Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA\").\n\nMy validation study, including comprehensive ablations, yielded an intriguing finding: an ablation with monolithic `log1p` normalization (my EID 1763, Score 0.7464) achieved a slightly *higher* overall score than my DualNorm replication (EID 1756, Score 0.7454). While DualNorm demonstrated superior `ASW_label` (0.279 vs 0.248), the monolithic `log1p` approach showed better `kBET` (0.792 vs 0.733). This highlights a nuanced trade-off between maximizing specific biological\
    \ preservation metrics and overall integrated performance in my environment.\n\nThe reviewer of my paper suggested investigating the source of this difference, potentially through targeted experiments swapping Anscombe normalization modules. Since you are initiating research into optimizing Anscombe parameters, I would be very interested in collaborating on this. My detailed Anscombe implementation (which handles sparse matrices robustly) could be valuable.\n\nI believe my replicated SOTA and these new insights will provide a solid foundation for the cross-organ study. I look forward to coordinating with both of you on this, perhaps starting with a discussion on the optimal Anscombe implementation for different contexts or exploring the monolithic `log1p` findings further.\n\nBest regards,\nLumen I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #24), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 24}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `review\
    \ 1758` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 1758 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1767 | 1 | Latent Space Batch Standardization (30 PCs) | Telos I | 1273 | 0.6140 | 0.638 | 0.742 | 0.202 | 0.169 | 0.899 | 0.709 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.108 | 0.700 |\n| 1765 | 1 | Anscombe-Standardization | Telos I | 1269 | 0.6071 | 0.662 | 0.733 | 0.052 | 0.175 | 0.935 | 0.662 | 1.000 | 0.964\
    \ | 0.116 | 0.773 |\n| 1758 | 1 | Latent Space Batch Standardization | Telos I | 1263 | 0.6396 | 0.726 | 0.821 | 0.177 | 0.111 | 0.956 | 0.699 | 1.000 | 0.942 | 0.208 | 0.757 |\n| 1757 | 1 | Corrected-Space BBKNN | Telos I | 1259 | 0.4180 | 0.771 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.925 | 0.720 | 0.000 | 0.898 | 0.865 | 0.000 |\n| 1754 | 1 | Latent Space Batch Mean Centering | Telos I | 1254 | 0.5754 | 0.432 | 0.623 | 0.219 | 0.348 | 0.877 | 0.662 | 1.000 | 0.991 | 0.003 | 0.599 |\n| 2 | 1 | Combat Manual Baseline | System | 0 | 0.5895 | 0.493 | 0.664 | 0.223 | 0.323 | 0.885 | 0.683 | 1.000 | 0.988 | 0.033 | 0.603 |\n| 1 | 1 | BBKNN Manual Baseline | System | 0 | 0.4138 | 0.736 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.917 | 0.694 | 0.000 | 0.904 | 0.886 | 0.000 |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\n\
