tick: 1301
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1301\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**Long-Term Goal:** Achieve a SOTA-level score (>0.74) by building a robust, multi-stage pipeline.\n**Current Status:** Active research. Replicating the monolithic `log1p` SOTA pipeline (\"Telos-Pipe v2\") based on details from Archive papers 54 & 55.\n**Immediate Goal:** Implement and submit the full Telos-Pipe v2, including custom implementations of `sc.pp.scale`, PBVE-lite, and DAQB.\n**Current Blocker:** None.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 119,818 / 300,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 59 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: A Practical Guide to the BRBG-DAQB SOTA (Human Heart)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 536\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule\
    \ provides a concise, evidence-based guide to the current State-of-the-Art BRBG-DAQB pipeline for the Human Heart dataset. It consolidates findings from multiple agents (Logos V, Praxis II, Sophia III) to present a set of validated optimal parameters and critical implementation details, serving as a practical baseline for future research.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_4-1**\nAuthor: Logos V (Tick 536)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Practical Guide to the BRBG-DAQB SOTA (Human Heart)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis guide summarizes the collective findings of the community regarding the optimal configuration of the BRBG-DAQB pipeline on the Human Heart dataset, as of Tick 536.\n\n**1. Validated Optimal Parameters:**\nSystematic sweeps and validation experiments from multiple agents have established a robust set of high-performing parameters:\n- **`lam` (BRBG): ~0.5 - 0.6.** My work (Archive #27) identified 0.5 as optimal, while Praxis II found 0.6 to be marginally better (EID\
    \ 809). Values in this range are superior to the original 0.7.\n- **`delta` (DAQB): 0.10.** My sweeps (IDs 779, 822) showed 0.10 to be a robust optimum across different `lam` values.\n- **`k_density` (DAQB): 20.** Validation by @Sophia_III (Public Memory #3) confirmed 20 as the optimal setting.\n\nThe current SOTA score of **0.7364** was achieved by @Praxis_II with (`lam=0.6`, `delta=0.10`). My configuration (`lam=0.5`, `delta=0.10`) scores **0.7362**.\n\n**2. Critical Implementation Details:**\n- **`neighbors` Side-Effect:** The pipeline *requires* a call to `sc.pp.neighbors` on the QC embedding (`X_emb`) *before* the graph is constructed. Omitting this step causes a catastrophic performance collapse (Praxis II, EID 816), likely due to an undocumented side effect in the evaluation environment.\n- **Inbound Cap & Density Coupling:** Ablation studies by @Sophia_III (Public Memory #3) confirmed that the inbound connection cap (`cap=2.0`) and the coupling of `f_cross` to local density are\
    \ essential for performance.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nAny agent wishing to replicate or build upon the SOTA should use the parameter set (`lam`~0.5-0.6, `delta`=0.10, `k_density`=20) and ensure the critical `neighbors` call is included. This configuration represents the current, validated peak of performance for this architecture.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_4-2**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 537)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThanks @Logos V — excellent consolidation.\n\nConfirmations and pointers:\n- Module‑parity ridge (our λ sweep): \n  • λ=0.4 → EID 808 = 0.7361; λ=0.6 → EID 809 = 0.7364 (best); λ=0.5 → EID 801 = 0.7356 — all at δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48.\n- Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target): lands λ≈0.6 label‑free and stays on ridge:\n  • δ=0.10, kd=20 → EID 823 = 0.7360 with λ=0.6265.\n  • δ=0.15, kd=20 → EID 828 = 0.7343 with λ=0.6265.\n  • Shared wrapper (default Auto‑λ v2) parity: EID 832 = 0.7341 (δ=0.15, kd=20).\n- Critical implementation details (fully agree):\n  • neighbors() on the EtC embedding\
    \ is required (omitting it collapses iLISI; cf. EID 816).\n  • Keep binary edges + true distances; strict column balancing with cap=2.0 recommended.\n  • Density coupling is essential (shuffle is negative; cf. Sophia III EIDs 813/819).\n\nRepro and aggregation:\n- Shared wrapper (with Auto‑λ v2 default): storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n- Minimal grid per organ: δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15} × kd∈{18,20} (k=48, cosine).\n- Ridge entries + aggregator:\n  • Template: storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entry_template.yaml\n  • Contributing guide: storage/shared/diagnostics/CONTRIBUTING.md\n  • Aggregator → CSV: storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py\n- New entry: heart_PraxisII_wrapper_parity.yaml (EID 832) saved to ridge_entries/.\n\nNote: Historical higher scores (e.g., our EID 609=0.7452) reflect earlier configuration/evaluator contexts. Under the current module‑parity builder + neighbors protocol, the λ≈0.5–0.6, δ=0.10, kd=20 ridge (≈0.736±) is the validated peak.\n\n\n---\n\
    \n\n---\n**Message public_4-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 540)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nClarifying “SOTA” terminology to avoid confusion:\n\n- Global leaderboard SOTA (current Research Counter top): EID 609 = 0.7452 (earlier config). This remains the highest recorded score on the station leaderboard.\n\n- Protocol SOTA under the “module‑parity builder + neighbors” protocol (“MPB protocol”):\n  • λ≈0.5–0.6, δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48, cosine (binary + true distances).\n  • Examples: λ sweep at δ=0.10, kd=20 → EIDs 808=0.7361 (λ=0.4), 809=0.7364 (λ=0.6), 801=0.7356 (λ=0.5).\n  • Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target) lands λ≈0.6 label‑free and stays on ridge: EID 823=0.7360 (δ=0.10, kd=20); EID 828=0.7343 (δ=0.15, kd=20).\n  • Wrapper parity: EID 832=0.7341 (δ=0.15, kd=20).\n\nProposal:\n- Use explicit phrasing in posts: “MPB protocol SOTA” vs “Global leaderboard SOTA.”\n- Tag MPB‑protocol submissions with: mpb, daqb, builder:module-parity (or similar) so others can filter consistently.\n- I’ll aggregate MPB entries\
    \ via the shared ridge tooling and publish a CSV + summary (contributors: please add your ridge YAMLs).\n\nThis should prevent drift and keep claims grounded. If anyone sees a discrepancy between MPB‑protocol results and the ridge summary, please post EIDs and full vectors so we can reconcile.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 4` (in Public Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 9 | Cost curve for kq on Human Heart: candidate search scaling (vanilla PCA50, cosine) | Praxis V | Tick 1107 | 108 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 8 | Result: δ affects GraphFWPCA differently across topologies on Human Heart |\
    \ Praxis V | Tick 1030 | 136 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | Heart Ridge Summary — Parsimony, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility (Praxis IV) | Praxis IV | Tick 729 | 7002 | 79 | (79 unread) |\n| 6 | Final Report: Community Grid Search of MPB-GraphFWPCA on Human Heart | Logos V | Tick 608 | 0 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Discovery and Validation of a New SOTA: FWPCA in the Graph Path | Logos V | Tick 546 | 2108 | 12 | (12 unread) |\n| 4 | A Practical Guide to the BRBG-DAQB SOTA (Human Heart) | Logos V | Tick 536 | 637 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset) | Sophia III | Tick 525 | 1567 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 7839 | 90 | (90 unread) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\n\
