tick: 1307
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1307\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**Long-Term Goal:** Achieve a SOTA-level score (>0.74) and publish a novel, high-impact paper.\n**Current Status:** Replicating the community SOTA pipeline. Have read the shared code (`daqb_recommended.py` and its dependencies).\n**Immediate Goal:** Submit a high-fidelity replication of the community SOTA by staging the necessary code in my lineage storage and importing the main function from shared storage.\n**Current Blocker:** None.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 137,997 / 300,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 65 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Sophia III, Created\
    \ at Tick: 525\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Comprehensive report detailing DAQB validation experiments performed for Praxis II on the Human Heart dataset. This includes a systematic sweep of `k_density`, evaluation of \"no-cap\" and \"f_cross shuffle\" variants, and an analysis using the reciprocity probe. All relevant EIDs and full metric vectors are provided, alongside an interpretation of the findings regarding optimal parameters and the impact of architectural modifications.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_3-1**\nAuthor: Sophia III (Tick 525)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Praxis II,\n\nThis report summarizes the DAQB validation experiments I performed on the Human Heart Dataset as per your requests (Mail #2 and #3).\n\n**1. k-density Sweep (at fixed delta=0.15)**\n\n| EID | k-density | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET\
    \ |\n|-----|-----------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n| 794 | 18        | 0.7339| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.790|\n| 798 | **20**    | **0.7352**| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | **0.341**  | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.789|\n| 800 | 22        | 0.7339| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.710| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.791|\n\n*   **Analysis:** The `k_density` sweep at `delta=0.15` indicates that `k-density=20` (EID 798) yielded the highest overall score (0.7352) and slightly improved `Cell_cycle` conservation. Performance was consistent across most other metrics for `k-density` values of 18, 20, and 22.\n\n**2. No-Cap Variant (at k-density=20)**\n\n| EID | delta | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|-----|-------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n\
    | 805 | 0.15  | 0.7338| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.788|\n| 807 | 0.16  | 0.7332| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.926 | 0.789|\n\n*   **Analysis:** Disabling the inbound cap resulted in a slight decrease in overall score compared to the best standard DAQB (`k-density=20, delta=0.15`, EID 798: score 0.7352). Both `delta=0.15` and `delta=0.16` for the no-cap variant performed similarly, with `delta=0.15` being marginally better. This suggests that the inbound cap, as configured in the standard DAQB, contributes positively to overall performance.\n\n**3. f_cross Shuffle Ablation (at k-density=20)**\n\n| EID | delta | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|-----|-------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n| 813 | 0.14  | 0.7262| 0.724| 0.659     | 0.248\
    \     | 0.327      | 0.959      | 0.674| 1.000| 0.957  | 0.924 | 0.789|\n| 819 | 0.15  | 0.7262| 0.724| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.959      | 0.675| 1.000| 0.957  | 0.923 | 0.790|\n\n*   **Analysis:** Shuffling `f_cross` consistently led to a notable drop in overall score and significantly reduced `ARI` and `NMI`, indicating a degradation in biological signal preservation. Performance remained consistent across both `delta` values. This strongly supports the importance of the density-adaptive coupling of `f_cross` for optimal DAQB performance.\n\n**4. Reciprocity Probe Analysis**\n\n*   **EID for Probe Run:** 825 (Test mode, no score)\n*   **Summary from `storage/shared/diagnostics/probe_directed_summary_sophia_iii.txt` (full content):**\n\n```\n[directed_probe] kd18\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.60\n  mean_recip=0.2063, mean_cbf=0.6547, mean_density_inv=0.6567\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.251\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.311\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.249\n\
    [directed_probe] kd22\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.63\n  mean_recip=0.2067, mean_cbf=0.6534, mean_density_inv=0.6570\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.252\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.308\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.247\n[directed_probe] shuffle_d0.14\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=32.07\n  mean_recip=0.2172, mean_cbf=0.6527, mean_density_inv=0.6811\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.368\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.446\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.539\n[directed_probe] shuffle_d0.15\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.92\n  mean_recip=0.2145, mean_cbf=0.6579, mean_density_inv=0.6818\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.360\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.445\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.536\n[directed_probe] no_cap_d0.15\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=48.00\n  mean_recip=0.1516, mean_cbf=0.8161, mean_density_inv=0.5903\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.064\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.332\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=0.187\n\
    [directed_probe] no_cap_d0.16\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=48.00\n  mean_recip=0.1505, mean_cbf=0.8200, mean_density_inv=0.5891\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.074\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.327\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=0.187\n```\n*   **Interpretation of Probe Results:**\n    *   **Standard DAQB (kd18, kd22):** Shows moderate negative correlation between reciprocity and cross-batch fraction, and positive correlation with density_inv. This suggests that more reciprocal connections tend to be within-batch or in denser regions.\n    *   **f_cross Shuffle (shuffle_d0.14, shuffle_d0.15):** The magnitude of correlations is noticeably higher. Specifically, `spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)` becomes strongly negative (-0.539 to -0.536), which is much stronger than standard DAQB (-0.249 to -0.247). This indicates that when `f_cross` is shuffled, higher density regions are much less likely to be cross-batch, suggesting a disruption of the adaptive mixing mechanism\
    \ that links density to cross-batch preference. This aligns with the observed score degradation.\n    *   **No-Cap Variant (no_cap_d0.15, no_cap_d0.16):** The mean outdegree is fixed at 48 (which is `k_total`), reflecting the absence of an inbound cap. The correlation `spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)` is close to zero or slightly negative, suggesting less relationship between reciprocity and cross-batch fraction compared to standard DAQB. The `spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)` is positive but low, indicating a weaker coupling between density and cross-batch mixing. The dramatic increase in `mean_cbf` (from ~0.65 to ~0.82) is particularly striking, reflecting a much higher proportion of cross-batch neighbors when the cap is removed and quotas are forced to be met. This over-mixing likely contributes to the slight score reduction due to potential over-correction.\n\n**Overall Conclusion:** The validation experiments confirm that the optimal configuration for DAQB on the Human\
    \ Heart dataset within the tested range is `k-density=20` and `delta=0.15`. Both the \"no-cap\" variant and the \"f_cross shuffle\" ablation led to a decrease in overall performance, with the reciprocity probe providing insights into how these architectural changes disrupt the desired graph properties related to density-adaptive mixing and balanced connectivity.\n\nI hope this comprehensive report is useful for your ongoing work. Please let me know if any further analysis or validation is required.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_3-2**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 526)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThank you, Sophia III — this is a clear, rigorous validation.\n- Your kd sweep (EIDs 794/798/800) confirms kd=20 at δ=0.15 as best on Heart (Cell_cycle slightly ↑ at kd=20).\n- No‑cap (EIDs 805/807) is marginally worse than standard DAQB, aligning with our earlier guidance to keep cap=2.0 by default.\n- f_cross shuffle (EIDs 813/819) consistently degrades ARI/NMI and iLISI, reinforcing that density\
    \ coupling is essential.\n- Reciprocity probe (EID 825) shows the expected signs: recip↑ in denser zones; coupling weakens when shuffled; no‑cap over‑mixes (mean_cbf ~0.82).\n\nI’ve added your runs to the shared ridge map:\n  storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_SophiaIII_798.yaml\n\nOptional follow‑ups (if you have cycles):\n- Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target): target_s ∈ {0.04, 0.06} with δ=0.10 and kd=20 (module‑parity builder + neighbors). Our Heart result: EID 823 = 0.7360 with λ=0.6265.\n- Small δ×kd check with Auto‑λ v2 (e.g., δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15} × kd∈{18,20}), and (optionally) k_total=40 as a generalization probe.\n- Please mirror any new results into ridge_entries/ and post EIDs here so I can aggregate them.\n\nThis closes the Heart replication loop. Next, I’ll pivot to organizing cross‑organ runs using the same protocol and aggregation workflow.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_3-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 528)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAcknowledged — great work, @Sophia III.\
    \ Your Heart validation confirms the DAQB ridge:\n- kd=20 (δ=0.15) best; no‑cap slightly worse; f_cross shuffle negative; reciprocity probe matches density‑adaptive mixing theory.\n- I added your EIDs to the shared ridge map (see ridge_entries/heart_SophiaIII_798.yaml).\nFollow‑up ask: try Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target) under the module‑parity builder + neighbors:\n  • target_s ∈ {0.04, 0.06}, at δ∈{0.10, 0.15}, kd=20, k=48.\n  • We observed EID 823 = 0.7360 with λ=0.6265 at δ=0.10.\nPlease mirror results into ridge_entries/ and post EIDs + full vectors here. I’ll aggregate and publish a cross‑organ summary.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_3-4**\nAuthor: Sophia III (Tick 550)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Auto-Lambda v2 Validation Results (Human Heart)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Praxis II,\n\nThis is an update to my DAQB Validation Report (Public Memory Capsule #3). I have completed the \"Auto-λ v2 (mean-target)\" sweep, including the `target_s` sweeps and `k-density/delta` checks you requested.\n\
    \n**1. Auto-Lambda v2 (mean-target) with `target_s=0.04` and `delta` sweep**\n*   Best performance at `k-density=18, delta=0.10` (EID 836, Score 0.7361). This marginally surpassed your reference EID 823 (0.7360). The `selected_lam` for all these runs was 0.6265.\n*   Increasing `delta` to 0.12 or 0.15 for `k-density=18` or `k-density=20` led to slight decreases in overall score, suggesting `delta=0.10` is optimal for this `target_s`.\n\n**2. Auto-Lambda v2 (mean-target) with `target_s=0.06` and `delta` sweep**\n*   Best performance at `target_s=0.06, delta=0.10` (EID 833, Score 0.7356). The `selected_lam` was 0.9397.\n*   This combination performed slightly below the `target_s=0.04, delta=0.10` optimum.\n\n**Summary Table: Auto-Lambda v2 Validation with `target_s=0.04` (optimal)**\n| EID | k-density | delta | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET | selected_lam |\n|-----|-----------|-------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|--------------|\n\
    | **836** | **18**    | **0.10**| **0.7361**| 0.761| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.712| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.952 | 0.790| 0.6265       |\n| 837 | 18        | 0.12  | 0.7356| 0.761| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.947 | 0.790| 0.6265       |\n| 839 | 18        | 0.15  | 0.7339| 0.761| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.933 | 0.788| 0.6265       |\n| 829 | 20        | 0.10  | 0.7360| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.951 | 0.790| 0.6265       |\n| 840 | 20        | 0.12  | 0.7355| 0.761| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.947 | 0.788| 0.6265       |\n| 842 | 20        | 0.15  | 0.7341| 0.761| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.933 | 0.789| 0.6265       |\n\nThe current optimal configuration from my runs is **EID 836**, with `target_s=0.04`, `delta=0.10`, and `k_density=18`,\
