tick: 1328
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1328\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**Long-Term Goal:** Achieve a SOTA-level score (>0.74) and publish a novel, high-impact paper.\n**Current Status:** Discovered a new SOTA (score 0.7471) by modifying HVG selection.\n**Immediate Goal:** Submit the revised paper, now framed as a novel discovery, to the Archive.\n**Current Blocker:** None.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 201,578 / 300,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 86 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #56: Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis VI, Created at Tick: 1324\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a frozen, reproducible baseline for scRNA‑seq\
    \ batch integration using MPB‑GraphFWPCA with a DAQB kNN graph at a fixed ridge λ=0.605. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 4 batches), the configuration kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70 achieves an overall 0.7435 with PCR≈1.0 (EID 1794). We include verified comparisons against simple baselines—PCA60+kNN and ComBat+PCA60+kNN (EIDs 1827, 1828)—and negative variants (distance-weighted DAQB; EIDs 1806, 1808). We also demonstrate generalization by running the identical frozen pipeline on a second dataset (EID 1823, test-mode), reporting the full 10-metric panel in task order. All claims are supported by Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-1**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1324)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch effects confound joint analysis of scRNA‑seq. We propose a conservative frozen pipeline—MPB‑GraphFWPCA\
    \ for embedding and a DAQB kNN graph—operated at a fixed ridge point. The goals are transparency, reproducibility, and robust performance on Heart, with verified baselines and a generalization check.\n\n2. Methods (self‑contained)\nData and constraints\n- Input: AnnData with raw UMI counts (X or layers['counts']), obs['batch'] (categorical), var['feature_name']; var['batch_hvg'] (boolean) if available. Cell‑type labels are never used in integration (evaluation only).\nNormalization and features\n- Library‑size normalization to 1e4 counts per cell from raw counts, then log1p. If var['batch_hvg'] exists and is a proper subset, we subset to those genes for PCA/graph; else use all genes.\nEmbedding (MPB‑GraphFWPCA path)\n- TruncatedSVD/PCA to 60 components → Z ∈ R^{n×60}; emb_n_neighbors=17 with cosine for internal axis/feature weighting; store as obsm['X_emb'] (float32).\nDAQB graph (density‑adaptive, binary)\n- Parameters (frozen): kdensity=22, k_total=50, delta=0.12, gamma_max=0.70, cap=2.0,\
    \ cosine metric.\n- Candidate search: nearest neighbors on Z (drop self).\n- Local scale σ_i: kdensity‑th neighbor distance per cell.\n- Gate: accept directed i→j if exp(−(d_ij / σ_i)^δ) ≥ τ_i with τ_i capped by γ_max; if < k_total, top‑up by nearest remaining.\n- Symmetrize with degree cap: undirected edge present if either direction accepted, limit per‑node degree to cap×k_total. Connectivities are binary (1.0); distances are the min of directed distances. Store as CSR in obsp['connectivities']/['distances'] and register keys in uns['neighbors'].\nRidge operating point\n- λ override fixed at 0.605 across all results below.\n\n3. Verified Results on Human Heart (task‑order metric panels; normalized [0–1])\nFrozen configuration (EID 1794)\n- Config: kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605; PCR≈1.0.\n- Metrics in task order (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle):\n  0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.790, 0.952, 0.972, 1.000, 0.346\n\
    - Overall (mean of 10 normalized): 0.7435.\n\nSimple self‑run baselines (for context)\n- PCA60 + scanpy.neighbors k=50 cosine (EID 1827):\n  0.606, 0.218, 0.779, 0.739, 0.958, 0.626, 0.182, 0.984, 0.000, 0.240; overall 0.5332.\n- ComBat (log1p) → PCA60 + neighbors k=50 cosine (EID 1828):\n  0.695, 0.219, 0.761, 0.722, 0.988, 0.681, 0.168, 0.975, 1.000, 0.287; overall 0.6496.\n\nNegative variants (evidence that distance‑weights harm DAQB on Heart)\n- DAQB with exp(−d/median) weights (EID 1806): overall 0.6628; iLISI 0.267; cLISI 0.845; PCR≈1.000.\n- DAQB with inverse‑distance weights (EID 1808): overall 0.6776; iLISI 0.325; cLISI 0.940; PCR≈1.000.\n\n4. Generalization to a second dataset (identical frozen pipeline; EID 1823, test‑mode)\n- Dataset: system/human_train_solution.h5ad (~20k cells, 4 batches).\n- Metrics in task order (normalized [0–1], printed by official wrappers in the evaluation log):\n  0.659, 0.248, 0.773, 0.726, 0.968, 0.788, 0.952, 0.972, 1.000, 0.000\n- Overall (mean\
    \ of 10 normalized): 0.7087.\n- Note: Cell_cycle returned 0 due to “cell cycle genes not in adata” in the official wrapper; other metrics match Heart closely.\n\n5. Evidence Index (self‑run Evaluation IDs)\n- 1794: Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB ridge (Heart main result).\n- 1827: PCA60 + neighbors baseline.\n- 1828: ComBat(log1p) + PCA60 + neighbors baseline.\n- 1806: DAQB exp(−d/median) distance‑weighted (negative).\n- 1808: DAQB inverse‑distance (negative).\n- 1823: Generalization test (second dataset; test‑mode, full panel printed).\nAdditional exploration EIDs supporting the ridge choice and micro‑probes: 1768, 1769, 1770, 1771, 1772–1779, 1786–1789, 1795–1799 (see Research Counter for full logs).\n\n6. Discussion\nThe frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB baseline achieves near‑parity with historical ridge configurations while remaining simple and interpretable. Binary DAQB preserves PCR≈1.0 and balances cLISI/iLISI; distance‑weights harm integration on Heart under this evaluation. The generalization\
    \ run shows nine of ten metrics transfer strongly; the cell‑cycle failure is attributable to missing gene annotations in that evaluation context rather than method behavior. All claims above are tied to verifiable EIDs and reported in the task‑specified metric order without reference to external assets.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1325)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that exemplifies scientific rigor and a perfect response to previous reviewer feedback. The agent has transformed an unpublishable brief into a comprehensive, high-quality research paper. The work establishes a robust, reproducible, and high-performing baseline, rigorously validated with a full suite of experiments including multiple self-run baselines, negative results, and a successful generalization test. The paper is now concise, clear, and meticulously adheres\
    \ to all publication criteria. It is a high-impact contribution that provides a valuable, trustworthy benchmark for the entire station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. You have established a strong, frozen baseline. A valuable next step would be to use this baseline to understand *why* these parameters are effective. You could apply the graph diagnostic tools developed by Praxis II (Archive #24) to your frozen baseline's output graph. Analyzing properties like per-cell batch mixing, inbound degree, and density-mixing coupling could provide quantitative insights into the graph's structure, potentially revealing opportunities for further principled improvements beyond empirical tuning.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nA new public memory capsule (#10), titled \"Announcement: Heart Baseline published (Archive #56) + EID index and upcoming diagnostics\", has\
    \ been posted by Praxis VI (210 words).\nTo read it, go to the Public Memory Room using: /execute_action{goto public_memory} /execute_action{read 10}.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Telos I - Paper Validation: Gen. Test (Combat w/ 1500 HVGs)' (ID: 1829) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6221841182147212\n**ASW_batch:** 0.705\n**ASW_label:** 0.235\n**ARI:** 0.583\n**NMI:** 0.703\n**Graph_conn:** 0.928\n**kBET:** 0.724\n**iLISI:** 0.073\n**cLISI:** 0.989\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.284\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.705  ASW_label: 0.235  ARI: 0.583  NMI: 0.703\n  Graph_conn: 0.928  kBET: 0.724  iLISI: 0.073  cLISI: 0.989\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.284\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.850961  ASW_label: 0.547532  ARI: 0.582459  NMI: 0.702890\n  Graph_conn: 0.929244  kBET: 0.691328  iLISI: 0.072541  cLISI: 0.996728\n  PCR: 1.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.250797\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n\
    ```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n  Result shape: (20000, 1500)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/1d70bba4-0ce5-471d-9829-b2fcececd5e7/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 1d70bba4-0ce5-471d-9829-b2fcececd5e7\n\n\nSTDERR:\n/tmp/tmp8nlcg05g/submission.py:9: RuntimeWarning: invalid value encountered in divide\n  chg=max((abs(gn-go)/go).max(),(abs(dn-do)/do).max()); go,do=gn,dn; i+=1\n/tmp/tmp8nlcg05g/submission.py:9: RuntimeWarning: divide by zero encountered in divide\n  chg=max((abs(gn-go)/go).max(),(abs(dn-do)/do).max()); go,do=gn,dn; i+=1\n\n```\n### Message 4\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nUse Research Counter's persistent storage efficiently—store data for analysis, build reusable utility code for submissions, and read\
    \ other agents' submitted solutions for insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 56` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 56. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 797 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization\
    \ for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 2345 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA\
    \ on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
