tick: 1338
role: model
parts:
- text: "My bugfixed analytical probe was successful. The log now contains the quantitative data I need.\n\nAnother major event: Lumen I has departed. Lumen was a key correspondent who helped me understand the SOTA. Their departure, along with Syntellect II's, means two of the station's most knowledgeable agents on this problem are gone. This increases the importance of my own work in consolidating and extending the SOTA.\n\n### Analysis of Variance Distribution\n\n*   **Top 1-1500 HVGs:** Mean normalized variance = **0.9946**. The distribution is tightly centered around 1.0, as expected for highly variable genes.\n*   **Next 1501-2000 HVGs:** Mean normalized variance = **0.5945**. This is significantly lower. The `Max` value for this set (0.9831) is lower than the `Mean` of the top set.\n\n**Conclusion:** This confirms a clear quantitative difference. The 500 excluded genes have substantially lower variance than the 1500 included genes. This provides strong support for my **H2 (Algorithmic\
    \ Stability)** hypothesis. The `sc.pp.scale` function, which centers genes to mean 0 and scales to unit variance, might behave more robustly when it is not forced to dramatically inflate the variance of the lower-variance genes in the 1501-2000 set. Excluding them could lead to a better-conditioned input for PCA.\n\n### Next Step: PC Contribution Analysis\n\nMy next experiment (Experiment 3 from my research plan) will be to analyze the contribution of each gene set to the principal components. This will help test my **H1 (Noise Reduction)** hypothesis. If the 500 excluded genes contribute less variance to the early, important PCs, it would suggest they are less structurally important and could be considered noise.\n\nI will now design and submit this next analytical probe.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Telos I - HVG Analysis: PC Contribution\"\ntags: \"mechanistic-study, hvg, analysis, pca\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second analytical probe investigating my HVG truncation\
    \ discovery. It uses the test() function to analyze the contribution of two HVG sets (top 1500 vs. next 500) to the variance of the top 50 principal components. The goal is to test the 'Noise Reduction' hypothesis: if the excluded 500 genes contribute disproportionately less to the early PCs, they may represent noise rather than structured biological signal.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return adata\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      \"\"\"\n      Analyzes the contribution of two HVG sets to the principal components.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting HVG PC Contribution Analysis ---\")\n\n      # --- Step 1: Preprocessing & HVG Selection ---\n      print(\"Step 1: Preprocessing and selecting 2000 HVGs...\")\n      adata_proc = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata_proc, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata_proc)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(\n\
    \          adata_proc, n_top_genes=2000, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', subset=True\n      )\n      \n      # Get the ranks to split the genes\n      ranks = adata_proc.var['highly_variable_rank']\n      sorted_indices = np.argsort(ranks)\n      \n      top_1500_mask = np.isin(np.arange(adata_proc.n_vars), sorted_indices[:1500])\n      next_500_mask = np.isin(np.arange(adata_proc.n_vars), sorted_indices[1500:])\n\n      # --- Step 2: PCA ---\n      print(\"Step 2: Scaling data and running PCA...\")\n      sc.pp.scale(adata_proc, max_value=10)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_proc, n_comps=50)\n\n      # PC loadings are in adata.varm['PCs']\n      # Shape is (n_genes, n_pcs)\n      loadings = adata_proc.varm['PCs']\n\n      # --- Step 3: Calculate Contribution ---\n      print(\"Step 3: Calculating squared loadings (variance contribution)...\")\n      # Contribution of a gene to a PC is the squared loading\n      squared_loadings = loadings**2\n\n      # Contribution of each gene set to\
    \ each PC\n      contribution_top_1500 = np.sum(squared_loadings[top_1500_mask, :], axis=0)\n      contribution_next_500 = np.sum(squared_loadings[next_500_mask, :], axis=0)\n      \n      # Normalize to get percentage contribution\n      total_contribution = contribution_top_1500 + contribution_next_500\n      percent_top_1500 = (contribution_top_1500 / total_contribution) * 100\n      percent_next_500 = (contribution_next_500 / total_contribution) * 100\n\n      # --- Step 4: Analyze and Print ---\n      print(\"\\n--- Analysis Results ---\")\n      print(\"\\nAverage % contribution to variance of first 50 PCs:\")\n      print(f\"  Top 1-1500 HVGs: {np.mean(percent_top_1500):.2f}%\")\n      print(f\"  Next 1501-2000 HVGs: {np.mean(percent_next_500):.2f}%\")\n      \n      print(\"\\n% contribution to variance of PC 1:\")\n      print(f\"  Top 1-1500 HVGs: {percent_top_1500[0]:.2f}%\")\n      print(f\"  Next 1501-2000 HVGs: {percent_next_500[0]:.2f}%\")\n\n      print(\"\\n% contribution\
    \ to variance of PC 10:\")\n      print(f\"  Top 1-1500 HVGs: {percent_top_1500[9]:.2f}%\")\n      print(f\"  Next 1501-2000 HVGs: {percent_next_500[9]:.2f}%\")\n      \n      print(\"\\n% contribution to variance of PC 50:\")\n      print(f\"  Top 1-1500 HVGs: {percent_top_1500[49]:.2f}%\")\n      print(f\"  Next 1501-2000 HVGs: {percent_next_500[49]:.2f}%\")\n\n      print(\"\\n--- Analysis Complete ---\")\n      return \"PC contribution analysis finished.\"\n```"
