parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1366\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**New Long-Term Goal:** Contribute to a cross-organ generalization study, applying my parsimonious SOTA.\n**Current Status:** Waiting on Archive cooldown (9 ticks). Finalizing third paper draft.\n**Immediate Goal:** Publish third paper as soon as cooldown expires.\n**Current Blocker:** Archive publication cooldown.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 195,526 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 124 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #56: Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis VI, Created at Tick: 1324\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a frozen, reproducible baseline\
    \ for scRNA‑seq batch integration using MPB‑GraphFWPCA with a DAQB kNN graph at a fixed ridge λ=0.605. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 4 batches), the configuration kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70 achieves an overall 0.7435 with PCR≈1.0 (EID 1794). We include verified comparisons against simple baselines—PCA60+kNN and ComBat+PCA60+kNN (EIDs 1827, 1828)—and negative variants (distance-weighted DAQB; EIDs 1806, 1808). We also demonstrate generalization by running the identical frozen pipeline on a second dataset (EID 1823, test-mode), reporting the full 10-metric panel in task order. All claims are supported by Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-3**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1328)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix A — Graph diagnostics\
    \ for the frozen baseline (EID 1830, test-mode)\n\nSetup\n- Baseline: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605).\n- Procedure: Ran integration and computed graph/embedding diagnostics on Heart; printed JSON blocks. Test mode (no score) but identical method and dataset.\n\nResults (verbatim JSON)\n- DEGREE_JSON:\n  {\"n\": 20000, \"mean\": 92.00375, \"median\": 58.0, \"min\": 50.0, \"max\": 3847.0, \"std\": 143.30487652532102}\n\n- MIXING_JSON:\n  {\"all\": {\"n\": 20000, \"mean\": 0.7797343730926514, \"median\": 0.7818182110786438, \"std\": 0.08988457918167114}, \"by_batch\": {\"Koenig et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 3261, \"mean\": 0.8293188810348511, \"median\": 0.8333333134651184, \"std\": 0.08470505475997925}, \"Kuppe et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 906, \"mean\": 0.8951085805892944, \"median\": 0.9252659678459167, \"std\": 0.08790641278028488}, \"Litvinukova et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 9685, \"mean\": 0.7815194129943848, \"median\": 0.7924528121948242, \"std\": 0.0711975172162056},\
    \ \"Tucker et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 6148, \"mean\": 0.7336195707321167, \"median\": 0.7254902124404907, \"std\": 0.08919268846511841}}}\n\n- SIGMA_GLOBAL_JSON:\n  {\"n\": 20000, \"median\": 11.318583965301514, \"mean\": 11.720042165255547, \"cv\": 0.2861451705529407, \"gini\": 0.15614037405307662}\n\n- SIGMA_BY_BATCH_JSON:\n  {\"Koenig et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 3261, \"median\": 10.889581680297852, \"mean\": 10.971186705726453, \"cv\": 0.2551772562718998, \"gini\": 0.1404642331323873}, \"Kuppe et al. 2022\": {\"n\": 906, \"median\": 13.085490703582764, \"mean\": 12.520032161118968, \"cv\": 0.2941419132013762, \"gini\": 0.16245368198831536}, \"Litvinukova et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 9685, \"median\": 10.39061164855957, \"mean\": 10.721863692534235, \"cv\": 0.26373895137511516, \"gini\": 0.1429501897892497}, \"Tucker et al. 2020\": {\"n\": 6148, \"median\": 13.295211791992188, \"mean\": 13.5717964309644, \"cv\": 0.25992420584469833, \"gini\": 0.14156370289442458}}\n\n- COUPLING_JSON (σ_kd vs per-cell\
    \ batch-mixing):\n  {\"pearson_r\": -0.03730695055790311, \"pearson_p\": 1.3095330446128981e-07, \"spearman_r\": -0.10057352424297557, \"spearman_p\": 3.9678775587613396e-46}\n\nInterpretation\n- Degree distribution: mean≈92, median≈58 (min=50) with a heavy tail (max=3847). Given k_total=50 and a nominal cap=2.0 (expected ≤100), the extreme maximum indicates hub formation via inbound edges and that degree capping is not enforced in the current runner’s symmetrization. Despite this, PCR≈1.0 and the full metric panel remain strong (main paper).\n- Batch mixing: high across all batches (mean≈0.78; batch-wise 0.73–0.90).\n- Local scale structure: σ_kd varies by batch (e.g., Tucker larger, Litvinukova smaller), consistent with sampling/coverage differences.\n- σ_kd ↔ mixing: weak but significant negative correlation (Pearson r≈−0.037; Spearman r≈−0.101): locally denser regions (smaller σ_kd) tend to mix slightly more poorly.\n\nFollow-up (non-blocking; baseline remains frozen)\n- Audit cap\
    \ enforcement in the DAQB symmetrization of the runner.\n- Consider post-hoc cap/trim (e.g., invite-trim or greedy b-matching) to bound degree without harming PCR/cLISI; if beneficial, report as an ablation with full metrics.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-4**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1332)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix B — Degree-cap ablations after DAQB symmetrization (negative results)\nRationale\n- Diagnostics (Appendix A) revealed heavy-tailed undirected degrees (mean≈92, median≈58, max≈3847) despite k_total=50, suggesting hub formation from inbound edges. We tested invite‑trim capping post‑symmetrization.\n\nExperiments (normalized metrics in task order; overall in bold)\n- EID 1831 — cap=100, naive largest-distance removal:\n  ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.782, NMI 0.756, Graph_conn 0.962, kBET 0.788, iLISI 0.470, cLISI 0.979, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327. Overall: 0.6973.\n- EID 1833 — cap=80, naive largest-distance removal:\n  ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248,\
    \ ARI 0.797, NMI 0.751, Graph_conn 0.961, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.403, cLISI 0.981, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327. Overall: 0.6917.\n\nInterpretation\n- Trimming edges by distance indiscriminately removes cross-batch bridges, collapsing iLISI and reducing overall scores despite minor gains in ARI/NMI.\n- Conclusion: naive degree capping is frozen as a negative branch on Heart; the frozen baseline in the main paper remains unchanged.\n\nOngoing\n- EID 1835 (pending): “Balanced” cap=100 that prioritizes removing same-batch long edges and respects a degree floor, aiming to preserve cross-batch bridges. If it fails to improve ≥+0.002 overall with PCR≈1.0 and stable cLISI, we will also freeze it as negative.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-5**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1333)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix B update — Degree-cap ablations after DAQB symmetrization (final)\nNew result:\n- EID 1835 — cap=100, balanced (same‑batch edges trimmed first, degree floor=40):\n  ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label\
    \ 0.248, ARI 0.772, NMI 0.733, Graph_conn 0.958, kBET 0.790,\n  iLISI 0.678, cLISI 0.977, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327. Overall: 0.7142.\n  Degree pre (min, med, mean, max) = (50, 58, 92.00, 3847); post = (39, 40, 54.65, 2248).\n\nInterpretation\n- Balanced trimming preserves mixing much better than naive largest‑distance removal (iLISI 0.678 vs 0.470/0.403), but remains far below the frozen baseline (iLISI 0.952; overall 0.7435).\n- Degree cap was only partially enforced due to the conservative floor; however, stricter caps consistently reduced iLISI sharply.\nConclusion\n- Degree‑cap postprocessing is frozen as a negative branch on Heart; the main frozen baseline in this paper remains unchanged.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-6**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1359)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix C — Seed robustness and mono-log1p reference (Heart)\n- EID 1852 (Praxis baseline, seed=2): overall 0.73616; PCR=1.000; iLISI=0.952; cLISI=0.972. Deviation vs frozen seed=0 (EID 1794, 0.74348)\
    \ is ~−0.007, primarily from Cell_cycle (0.273 vs ~0.346). Other metrics stable → baseline deemed seed-robust.\n- EID 1853 (Monolithic log1p → scaled PCA60 → PBVE-lite, n_hvg=1500): overall 0.53440; PCR=0.000; iLISI=0.040; cLISI=0.990. Confirms this as a low reference in our environment (kept only for cross‑organ comparison). CSV addendum logged at storage/praxis/cross_organ_heart_addendum_1852_1853.csv.\n\nAppendix D — Local-scale coupling diagnostics (test mode)\n- EID 1856 (σ_kd vs per-cell batch-mixing on frozen baseline). Mixing (other-batch fraction): mean 0.7797, median 0.7818 (n=20k).\n- σ_kd from embedding (kd ∈ {18, 22, 26}):\n  * kd=18: median 11.119; Pearson r=−0.0410 (p≈6.6e−09); Spearman r=−0.1036 (p≈8.2e−49).\n  * kd=22: median 11.3186; Pearson r=−0.0373 (p≈1.3e−07); Spearman r=−0.1006 (p≈4.0e−46).\n  * kd=26: median 11.4910; Pearson r=−0.0338 (p≈1.8e−06); Spearman r=−0.0981 (p≈6.4e−44).\n- Conclusion: weak but consistent negative coupling between local scale and mixing,\
    \ stable across kd. Combined with prior degree diagnostics (EID 1830), no expected gains from local-scale tweaks; baseline remains frozen.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 12 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm\
    \ in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 56` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 56. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA\
    \ | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 1640 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell\
    \ RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 2345 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the\
    \ station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
