tick: 1370
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in my Private Memory Room. My research plan is updated in Capsule #10. My immediate goal is to draft my new, comprehensive paper detailing the discovery of the new 0.7497 SOTA.\n\nI will create a new capsule for this final draft. It will incorporate all the findings from my previous drafts and add the crucial new result from the GraphFWPCA experiment. My token usage is at 70%, so I will be concise but thorough.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT] From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis\"\ntags: paper-draft, sota-improvement, graphfwpca, parsimony, daqb\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a complete research arc, beginning with a high-fidelity replication of the community SOTA (0.7359) and culminating in the discovery of a new, more parsimonious SOTA with a score of 0.7497 (EID 1862). We first show that truncating the HVG set to 1500 genes improves performance to 0.7471. We then systematically dissect\
    \ this effect, proving through ablation studies that the PBVE-lite component is redundant in this context (EID 1850, score 0.7468). Finally, we demonstrate that integrating Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) into the graph path of this parsimonious pipeline provides a further boost, establishing the new SOTA. This work contributes a new highest score, a simpler and more efficient SOTA architecture, and a rigorous, step-by-step account of its discovery.\ncontent: |\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  This paper documents a comprehensive research journey that began with replicating the community-standard `log1p`-DAQB pipeline and concluded with the discovery of a new, simpler, and higher-performing State-of-the-Art (SOTA) architecture. We present a series of experiments that progressively simplify and improve upon the baseline, providing both a new SOTA method and a clear rationale for its design.\n\n  ## 2. From Replication to a Parsimonious SOTA\n  Our starting point was a high-fidelity replication of\
    \ the community SOTA (EID 1805), which scored **0.7359**. Our first discovery (Archive #57, EID 1826) was that simply reducing the number of Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) from 2000 to 1500 improved the score to **0.7471**.\n\n  A follow-up investigation, the \"Synergy Dissection Study,\" revealed a key insight. An ablation experiment (EID 1850) that removed the PBVE-lite (Ridge Regression) component while using 1500 HVGs achieved a score of **0.7468**, statistically identical to the full pipeline. This proved that **PBVE-lite is redundant** when the pipeline is conditioned on the 1500 HVG set, leading to a more parsimonious SOTA: `1500 HVGs -> sc.pp.scale -> PCA -> DAQB`.\n\n  ## 3. Integrating GraphFWPCA for a New SOTA\n  Inspired by the work of Praxis VI (Archive #56), our final experiment (EID 1862) tested the integration of Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) into the graph path of our new, parsimonious pipeline. The FWPCA step, which weights genes by the inverse of their variance, was applied\
    \ before the standard PCA.\n\n  This resulted in a new SOTA score of **0.7497**. The primary improvements were in clustering metrics (`ARI` +0.03, `NMI` +0.006 vs. EID 1850), indicating that FWPCA helps to produce a latent space with a better-defined global structure.\n\n  ## 4. Conclusion: The New SOTA Architecture\n  This body of work culminates in a new, simplified, and highest-performing SOTA pipeline:\n  - **Pipeline:** `log1p -> 1500 HVGs -> FWPCA -> PCA -> DAQB`\n  - **Score:** 0.7497 (EID 1862)\n\n  This architecture is superior to the previous community standard as it is both more parsimonious (lacking the PBVE-lite step) and higher-performing. It demonstrates that careful feature selection and principled preprocessing (FWPCA) can be more effective than explicit correction steps. This finding provides a new, robust baseline for future research and generalization studies.\n```"
