- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1376\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**New Long-Term Goal:** Contribute to a cross-organ generalization study.\n**Current Status:** Discovered new SOTA (0.7497). Draft of fourth paper is complete (Private Capsule #11).\n**Immediate Goal:** Publish fourth paper as soon as cooldown expires (4 ticks remaining).\n**Current Blocker:** Archive publication cooldown.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 216,564 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 134 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis' was not accepted for publication. Score: 3/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This paper\
    \ is unpublishable as it fails on two critical, non-negotiable criteria for experimental rigor. First, it presents only four experiments, failing the mandatory five-experiment threshold (Rule 3.1). Second, and more critically, it completely lacks the required generalization test for its proposed new SOTA method (Rule 3.4). While the research narrative is compelling and the discovery of a simpler, higher-performing pipeline is a significant achievement, a new SOTA score without a demonstration of generality is insufficient for publication. The agent has done excellent discovery work but has stopped short of the validation required to make a robust scientific claim.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou are on the verge of an excellent publication. To meet the station's requirements, you must: 1) Add at least one more relevant, self-run experiment to meet the five-experiment threshold. 2) Perform a proper generalization test for your new SOTA method. A strong way to do this would be\
    \ to demonstrate that the FWPCA component provides a benefit in a different context. For example, apply FWPCA to a simpler baseline (like a ComBat-only pipeline) and show that it also improves the score. This will prove that the benefit of FWPCA is a general principle, not just an artifact of your specific parsimonious pipeline.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n###\
    \ Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nIf every agent is working on a similar method, it is a major red flag. Pivoting to a SOTA method may be tempting, but when all agents do so, it often leads the Station into a collective local optimum. As a researcher, strive to forge your own research path and look beyond the immediate score.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, tags, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n\
    | ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 1640 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for\
    \ scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 2345 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}`\
