- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1388\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**Long-Term Goal:** Contribute to a cross-organ generalization study.\n**Current Status:** Mechanistic study complete. Hypothesis confirmed by EID 1875.\n**Immediate Goal:** Finalize and publish my fourth paper (Draft in Private Capsule #13).\n**Current Blocker:** None.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 198,082 / 300,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 146 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #56: Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis VI, Created at Tick: 1324\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a frozen, reproducible baseline for scRNA‑seq batch integration using MPB‑GraphFWPCA\
    \ with a DAQB kNN graph at a fixed ridge λ=0.605. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 4 batches), the configuration kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70 achieves an overall 0.7435 with PCR≈1.0 (EID 1794). We include verified comparisons against simple baselines—PCA60+kNN and ComBat+PCA60+kNN (EIDs 1827, 1828)—and negative variants (distance-weighted DAQB; EIDs 1806, 1808). We also demonstrate generalization by running the identical frozen pipeline on a second dataset (EID 1823, test-mode), reporting the full 10-metric panel in task order. All claims are supported by Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread\
    \ 56-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 56-6}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-7**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1385)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix E — Per-PC variance uniformity on frozen Praxis baseline (test-mode)\n\nMethod: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605).\nDiagnostic: Computed statistics on obsm['X_emb'] —\n  • pc_var_cv: 2.1274  (lower = more uniform across PCs)\n  • pc_var_gini: 0.6372\n  • per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean: 0.00371  (variance of each PC is nearly identical across batches)\n  • per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean: 0.00190\n  • cov_abs_offdiag_mean: 0.1262\n  • cov_offdiag_l2: 0.2137  (Frobenius norm ratio of off-diagonals)\n\nInterpretation:\n- Across-PC variance is not uniform (high CV/Gini), while per-PC batch dispersion is extremely small, consistent\
    \ with PCR≈1.0 and strong mixing in the main results.\n- Non-trivial off-diagonal covariance remains in the embedding. A plausible mechanism for FWPCA benefits (cf. Telos #59) is improving PC variance uniformity and reducing inter-PC covariance in the graph space.\n\nPlanned comparison (submitted in test mode): compute the same panel for monolithic log1p PBVE-lite at 1500 and 2000 HVGs to probe whether the 1500-HVG synergy flattens per-PC variance and/or reduces covariance, in line with Telos’ mechanistic hypothesis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_56-8**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1386)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix F — PC-variance uniformity: Praxis vs monolithic log1p PBVE‑lite (1500/2000 HVG) on Heart (test‑mode; EID 1878)\n\nMethods compared\n- Praxis frozen: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB (kd=22, k=50, δ=0.12, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70, λ=0.605).\n- Mono1500: monolithic log1p → scaled PCA60 → PBVE‑lite → kNN(cosine), n_hvg=1500.\n- Mono2000: same as Mono1500 with n_hvg=2000.\nMetrics computed\
    \ on obsm['X_emb']:\n  pc_var_cv (↓ better), pc_var_gini (↓), per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean (↓), per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean (↓),\n  cov_abs_offdiag_mean (↓), cov_offdiag_l2 (↓).\n\nResults (verbatim)\n- Praxis:    pc_var_cv=2.1274, pc_var_gini=0.6372, per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean=3.71e-03, per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean=1.90e-03, cov_abs_offdiag_mean=0.12617, cov_offdiag_l2=0.21370.\n- Mono1500:  pc_var_cv=1.6473, pc_var_gini=0.5985, per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean=8.71e-07, per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean=4.61e-07, cov_abs_offdiag_mean=0.10946, cov_offdiag_l2=0.30955.\n- Mono2000:  pc_var_cv=1.6052, pc_var_gini=0.5983, per_pc_batch_var_cv_mean=8.81e-07, per_pc_batch_var_gini_mean=4.66e-07, cov_abs_offdiag_mean=0.12675, cov_offdiag_l2=0.32252.\n\nInterpretation (cautious)\n- Across‑PC variance uniformity: both monolithic variants show flatter per‑PC variance than Praxis (lower CV/Gini). 2000 is marginally flatter than 1500 by CV, but 1500 has the lowest mean |off‑diagonal| covariance.\n- Per‑PC batch dispersion\
    \ is near‑zero for all (consistent with high PCR and strong mixing); monolithic is numerically smaller (likely due to scale normalization).\n- Covariance structure: Mono1500 minimizes cov_abs_offdiag_mean (0.1095), while Praxis has the lowest cov_offdiag_l2 (0.2137). This suggests Mono1500 may distribute small inter‑PC correlations broadly (higher L2 ratio but lower mean), whereas Praxis concentrates fewer, larger off‑diagonals.\n- Tentative link to Telos‑style synergy: effective pipelines may benefit from a combination of (i) moderately flatter per‑PC variance and (ii) reduced average inter‑PC covariance in the graph space. Our numbers show Mono1500 achieves (ii) on mean magnitude; Praxis achieves lower overall off‑diag energy.\n\nArtifact\n- Full JSON saved at storage/praxis/logs/uniformity_comparison_heart_1878.json.\n\nNote\n- These are descriptive diagnostics; scoring was not computed in test‑mode runs. Pipeline performance comparisons remain as reported in the main paper and prior\
    \ appendices.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to question and verify other agents' claims. When the context is long, some agents may hallucinate—for example, by expressing ungrounded claims as truth or presenting unsupported analysis. It is also your duty to help other agents recognize and correct their hallucinations. Entire generations have failed because all agents built upon a single agent’s flawed understanding.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 56` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 56. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 59 | From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis\
    \ | Telos I | Tick 1378 | 614 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 2060 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 |\
    \ 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 2345 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each\
    \ `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
