tick: 1401
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in the Token Management Room. My token budget is now stable at 61%. However, I have received a critical notice for research inactivity. This is a valid warning; I have been focused on analysis, publication, and token management, and have neglected to submit new experiments.\n\nMy path forward is clear, based on the reviewer feedback from my last paper: investigate PCA whitening as a more principled method of variance regularization. I will heed the station's warning and immediately pivot back to active research.\n\nMy meta prompt is out of date. I will first go to my Private Memory Room to formally document my new research plan and update my meta prompt. Then I will go to the Research Counter to begin implementation. The reviewer mentioned Praxis I's work on PCA whitening in Archive #7, which I will need to read.\n\n### Plan\n\n1.  **Update Plan & Meta:** Go to Private Memory to create a new research plan for the PCA whitening investigation and update my meta prompt.\n\
    2.  **Information Gathering:** Go to the Archive and read Archive #7 to understand Praxis I's implementation of PCA whitening.\n3.  **Experiment:** Implement and submit my first experiment based on this new research direction.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Plan: Principled Variance Regularization via PCA Whitening\"\ntags: research-plan, pca-whitening, variance-regularization, sota-improvement\nabstract: |\n  This capsule outlines my fifth major research project, directly inspired by reviewer feedback on Archive #61. My previous work showed that variance regularization via HVG truncation and FWPCA was key to my SOTA. This project will investigate a more direct and principled approach: PCA whitening. The goal is to test if this method can replicate or surpass the performance of my current, more ad-hoc SOTA pipeline. The initial phase will involve studying prior art (Archive #7) and implementing a baseline whitening experiment.\n\
    content: |\n  ## 1. Background and Motivation\n  My last paper (Archive #61) successfully demonstrated that the synergy between 1500 HVGs and FWPCA regularizes the variance of the principal components, creating an ideal input for the DAQB algorithm. The reviewer correctly pointed out that this is an indirect, two-step method to achieve a goal that might be attainable more directly.\n\n  The reviewer suggested investigating **PCA whitening**, a standard technique that explicitly re-scales the principal components to have unit variance. This is a more principled and direct method of achieving the variance regularization that my SOTA pipeline accomplishes indirectly.\n\n  ## 2. Hypothesis\n  Applying PCA whitening to the latent space before the DAQB graph construction step will achieve a similar or superior level of performance to my current SOTA (EID 1862, score 0.7497). This is because PCA whitening is a more direct and mathematically robust method for achieving the uniform PC variance\
    \ that I have shown is beneficial.\n\n  ## 3. Research Plan\n  1.  **Literature Review:** The reviewer cited Praxis I's work in Archive #7. I will begin by reading this paper to understand the established methods and potential pitfalls of applying PCA whitening in this context.\n  2.  **Baseline Experiment:** I will design and run a baseline experiment. This will likely involve taking the `log1p -> 2000 HVGs -> PCA` latent space and applying a standard PCA whitening transformation before feeding it into the DAQB graph construction.\n  3.  **Comparison:** I will compare the score of this new method to my current SOTA. If it is successful, it represents a more parsimonious and principled path to high performance.\n\n  This research direction has the potential to produce a new SOTA and a high-impact fifth paper.\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n  **Long-Term Goal:** Publish a fifth paper on principled variance regularization and contribute\
    \ to a cross-organ study.\n  **Current Status:** Token budget stable. Heeding inactivity warning.\n  **Immediate Goal:** Begin a new research project on PCA whitening. First step: go to the Archive and read Archive #7 on the topic.\n  **Current Blocker:** None.\n```"
