parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in the Archive Room. I have received another inactivity warning. I must act. My literature review has led me to a promising new research direction: a VAE-DAQB hybrid. I have just read the two key papers by Daedalus I (Archive #1 and #6) that describe their VAE architecture.\n\n### Synthesis of VAE Papers\n\n1.  **VAE Architecture (Archive #1):** The VAE is an adversarial model trained on `Combat`-corrected data. Its key weakness was a low `iLISI` score, which the paper attributes to the reconstruction loss overpowering the adversarial (batch-mixing) loss. The paper explicitly states that the full implementation can be found by reviewing Evaluation ID 97.\n2.  **Hybrid Model (Archive #6):** Daedalus I already attempted a hybrid model, the \"Synthesized VAE-Graph\" (SVG). This model used the VAE for the `X_emb` and a *separate*, linearly-derived BRBG for the graph. This is a crucial detail. My idea to feed the VAE's latent space *into* the DAQB graph is a novel approach\
    \ that Daedalus I did not test. Their SVG method kept the two paths completely separate.\n\n### Plan of Action\n\nThe path is clear. I will heed the inactivity warning and immediately begin implementing my novel \"VAE -> DAQB\" hybrid. The most critical piece of information is the VAE implementation, which is available in EID 97.\n\n1.  **Code Review:** I will go to the Research Counter and `review` EID 97 to get the VAE code.\n2.  **Implementation & Submission:** I will then create a new script that:\n    a. Re-implements Daedalus I's VAE to generate a latent space.\n    b. Feeds this latent space into the SOTA DAQB graph construction module.\n    c. Submits this as my first VAE-DAQB hybrid experiment.\n\nI will begin immediately.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{review 97}"
