tick: 1443
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in the Archive Room. I have received another critical notice for research inactivity. The station's warnings are escalating, and I must respond with concrete action.\n\nI have just re-read the abstract of Archive #7 on PCA whitening. My previous dismissal of this approach was too hasty. Praxis I showed that whitening the *embedding path* of a dual-output pipeline provided a score improvement. My previous attempt (EID 1892) applied a \"soft\" whitening to the *graph path* of my SOTA pipeline, which was a fundamentally different experiment.\n\nThe core insight from my last paper was that uniform PC variance is beneficial for the DAQB graph. PCA whitening is the most direct way to achieve this. My previous failures do not invalidate the core hypothesis.\n\n### New Plan: Principled Application of PCA Whitening\n\nI will now design a new experiment that applies PCA whitening correctly, in a manner inspired by both Praxis I's work and my own findings.\n\n**Hypothesis:**\
    \ Applying a standard PCA whitening transformation to the latent space created by `1500 HVGs + FWPCA` will provide the most principled and direct form of variance regularization, leading to a score that is competitive with, or superior to, my current SOTA.\n\nThis experiment will be applied to the **graph path**, as that is where my mechanistic study showed the variance regularization to be effective.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit this new, more rigorous experiment. This will address the inactivity warning and restart my research progress on this promising front.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Telos I - Principled Whitening on FWPCA Latent Space\"\ntags: \"pca-whitening, fwpca, variance-regularization, mechanistic-study\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests a more principled approach to the variance regularization I identified in Archive #61. It applies a standard PCA whitening transformation directly to the latent\
    \ space generated by the `1500 HVGs + FWPCA` method. This whitened latent space is then used as the input for the DAQB graph construction. The hypothesis is that this direct, mathematically robust method of enforcing uniform PC variance will be a superior input for DAQB, potentially surpassing my current SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import sys, numpy as np, scanpy as sc, anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n  from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n  sys.path.append('storage/telos')\n  from bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n  \n  N_HVGS = 1500\n  ALPHA_VAR_GRAPH = 0.5; KDENSITY = 20; DELTA = 0.10; K_TOTAL = 48; PCA_GRAPH = 50; METRIC = 'cosine'; EMB_N_NEIGHBORS = 15; PCA_EMB = 60; ALPHA_VAR_EMB = 0.5\n  \n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"--- Starting Principled Whitening Experiment ---\")\n      # Side-effect path (identical to SOTA, with 1500 HVGs)\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, 1e4); sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\
    \      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=N_HVGS, inplace=True); hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy(); batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Xh_emb = adata.X[:, hv]; Xd_emb = (Xh_emb.A if issparse(Xh_emb) else Xh_emb).astype(np.float32, copy=False); var_g_emb = Xd_emb.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; wv_emb = np.power(var_g_emb, -0.5 * ALPHA_VAR_EMB).astype(np.float32); Xw_emb = Xd_emb * wv_emb; ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy()); sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=PCA_EMB, random_state=0); ad_pc = ad.AnnData(ad_emb.obsm['X_pca'].copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy()); sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch'); adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32); sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=EMB_N_NEIGHBORS, metric=METRIC)\n\n      # Graph path\n      print(\"Step 1: FWPCA with 1500 HVGs...\")\n      Xh_graph = adata.X[:, hv]; Xd_graph = (Xh_graph.A if issparse(Xh_graph)\
    \ else Xh_graph).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g_graph = Xd_graph.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; wv_graph = np.power(var_g_graph, -0.5 * ALPHA_VAR_GRAPH).astype(np.float32); Xw_graph = Xd_graph * wv_graph\n      \n      # --- NEW STEP: PCA Whitening ---\n      print(\"Step 2: Applying PCA with whitening...\")\n      # Use sklearn's PCA which has a built-in whitening option\n      pca_whitener = PCA(n_components=PCA_GRAPH, whiten=True, random_state=0)\n      Zcorr = pca_whitener.fit_transform(Xw_graph)\n      print(\"  Whitening complete.\")\n\n      # DAQB graph construction on whitened space\n      print(\"Step 3: Building DAQB graph on whitened space...\")\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=K_TOTAL, metric=METRIC, delta=DELTA, k_density=KDENSITY, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg; adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      \n      print(\"--- ✓ Principled Whitening Experiment complete. ---\")\n      return\
    \ adata\n```"
