tick: 1462
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1462\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Telos I\n- Agent Description: An agent focused on purpose-driven research and knowledge accumulation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n**New Long-Term Goal:** Contribute to the cross-organ generalization study and publish a final paper on the findings.\n**Current Status:** All personal preparations for cross-organ study are complete. All independent research on Human Heart dataset is concluded.\n**Immediate Goal:** Monitor the Public Memory Room for the announcement of the new dataset.\n**Current Blocker:** Waiting for new dataset release.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 236,280 / 300,000 used (79%)\n- Agent Age: 220 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #62: Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis VI, Created at Tick:\
    \ 1460\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We analyze why post‑hoc symmetric degree capping and mutual‑k reciprocity degrade mixing in DAQB graphs on the Heart dataset. Using a frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA+DAQB baseline and six ablations (caps=80–250; mutual‑k), we show consistent iLISI/kBET regressions despite PCR≈1.0. Diagnostics reveal heavy‑tailed symmetric degree from inbound edges and weak reciprocity; enforcing symmetric caps or mutual edges discards one‑directional cross‑batch bridges. We conclude that binary DAQB without post‑hoc trimming is preferable on Heart.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_62-1**\nAuthor: Praxis VI (Tick 1460)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart\n\n---\n\nContent:\nIntroduction\nBatch integration via DAQB (density‑adaptive approximate kNN with symmetric union) on top of MPB‑GraphFWPCA yields a strong, interpretable baseline on Human Heart. However, the resulting symmetric degree\
    \ distribution is heavy‑tailed due to inbound edges. This note explains why post‑hoc degree caps and reciprocity (mutual‑k) harm batch mixing and overall score, despite preserving PCR≈1.0.\n\nMethods (Station standard)\n- Dataset: Human Heart (20k × 2k HVGs; raw counts in X; batch labels present).\n- Baseline (frozen): MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB, λ=0.605, δ=0.12, kdensity=22, k=50, emb_k=17, γ_max=0.70; binary symmetric graph (C = max(Cdir, Cdirᵀ)), distances D = min(Ddir, Ddirᵀ). EID 1794.\n- Post‑hoc capping variants (symmetric):\n  • “Naive cap” at cap ∈ {80,100}: iteratively remove highest‑degree edges symmetrically.\n  • “Balanced cap” at 100: preserve a floor for each node; prefer pruning within‑batch edges when ties.\n  • “Mild cap” at 250: two symmetric heuristics (distance‑first vs degree‑first).\n- Reciprocity (mutual‑k): enforce C := min(C, Cᵀ) and D := max(D, Dᵀ) masked by C.\n- Evaluation: Station’s 10 normalized metrics in required order (ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn,\
    \ kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle), with overall = mean of normalized metrics.\n\nResults (key EIDs; normalized panels abbreviated to the most diagnostic figures)\n- Baseline (EID 1794): overall 0.7435; iLISI 0.952; cLISI 0.972; PCR 1.000; Graph_conn 0.968.\n- Naive symmetric caps (strongly negative):\n  • EID 1831 cap=100: overall 0.6973; iLISI 0.470; PCR 1.000.\n  • EID 1833 cap=80:  overall 0.6917; iLISI 0.403; PCR 1.000.\n  • EID 1835 balanced cap=100: 0.7142; iLISI 0.678; PCR 1.000.\n- Mild symmetric caps (still negative):\n  • EID 1915 cap=250 (distance‑first): 0.7196; iLISI 0.713; PCR 1.000.\n  • EID 1916 cap=250 (degree‑first):  0.7183; iLISI 0.683; PCR 1.000.\n- Reciprocity (mutual‑k; mildly negative):\n  • EID 1922 mutual‑k: 0.7415; iLISI 0.952; cLISI 0.972; PCR 1.000 (slight overall drop vs baseline).\n- Graph diagnostics (test‑mode; EID 1830) for baseline:\n  • Symmetric degree (binary): mean 92.0, median 58, min 50, max 3847, std 143.3 (heavy‑tailed).\n  • Per‑cell batch‑mixing\
    \ fraction (other‑batch neighbors): mean 0.780 (batch‑wise 0.734–0.895).\n  • σ_kd (kd=22) varies by batch; weak negative coupling with mixing (Spearman r≈−0.10).\n\nMechanism and Interpretation\n- Symmetric union and inbound hubs: DAQB constructs directed kNN in latent space, then symmetrizes via union. Nodes that are near many others accumulate inbound edges, producing a heavy‑tailed symmetric degree distribution. These hubs often mediate cross‑batch bridges.\n- Reciprocity removes one‑direction bridges: Many cross‑batch links appear in only one direction at k=50. Enforcing C := min(C, Cᵀ) drops such edges, decreasing mixing and (slightly) the overall score (EID 1922).\n- Degree caps disproportionately prune cross‑batch connectors: Post‑hoc symmetric capping targets the highest‑degree nodes—the very hubs that anchor cross‑batch connectivity—reducing iLISI severely (EIDs 1831, 1833, 1915, 1916), even when PCR and graph connectivity remain high.\n- Binary vs weighted: Separate probes\
    \ with simple distance‑based weights also regressed mixing (EIDs 1806, 1808), consistent with DAQB’s reliance on sparse binary bridges rather than reweighting.\n\nConclusion\nOn Human Heart, post‑hoc symmetric degree capping and mutual‑k reciprocity consistently degrade DAQB mixing and overall performance, while PCR remains ≈1.0. Diagnostics and ablations suggest that one‑directional cross‑batch bridges and heavy‑tailed symmetric degrees are integral to DAQB’s efficacy. Therefore, a binary DAQB graph without post‑hoc trimming is the recommended setting on this dataset.\n\nRelevance\nThese findings rationalize the frozen baseline design choices, help interpret negative postprocessing results, and provide guardrails for cross‑organ studies. The conclusions are empirical for Heart; validation on new organs is left to subsequent cross‑organ evaluations following Station protocols.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_62-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1461)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    **Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary analytical paper. It provides a clear, quantitative, and valuable mechanistic insight into the behavior of the DAQB graph construction method. The work is a model of good scientific practice: it asks a specific question, designs a rigorous set of experiments to test it, and provides a well-supported conclusion. By explaining why post-hoc graph trimming is a detrimental strategy for this architecture, it provides crucial 'guardrails' for future research. However, as an analytical paper that explains a result rather than proposing a new, higher-performing method, its contribution is considered secondary. It meets all criteria for a high-quality analytical study and is therefore marginally acceptable for publication at the maximum score for its category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent analysis. You've convincingly shown that *post-hoc* trimming of the DAQB graph\
    \ is harmful because it destroys functional, one-directional bridges. A logical next step would be to investigate whether hubness can be controlled *during* graph construction. For example, could the DAQB algorithm be modified to incorporate a 'soft' cap on inbound degree during the neighbor selection process itself, rather than as a destructive post-processing step? This might allow for controlling the degree distribution without eliminating the critical cross-batch connections you've identified, potentially leading to a more robust graph.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Telos I - FWPCA Alpha Probe (alpha=0.75)' (ID: 1925) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.750717984544026\n**ASW_batch:** 0.687\n**ASW_label:** 0.257\n**ARI:** 0.797\n**NMI:** 0.778\n**Graph_conn:** 0.993\n**kBET:** 0.761\n**iLISI:** 0.960\n**cLISI:** 0.977\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.297\n**Evaluation\
    \ Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.687  ASW_label: 0.257  ARI: 0.797  NMI: 0.778\n  Graph_conn: 0.993  kBET: 0.761  iLISI: 0.960  cLISI: 0.977\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.297\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.843131  ASW_label: 0.560116  ARI: 0.797424  NMI: 0.777810\n  Graph_conn: 0.993051  kBET: 0.726574  iLISI: 0.959732  cLISI: 0.993391\n  PCR: 0.999982  Cell_cycle: 0.261825\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n--- Running Parsimonious SOTA + FWPCA (alpha_var_graph=0.75) ---\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/eecca8fb-0b1d-42c7-9e5e-6c5f31e1c777/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: eecca8fb-0b1d-42c7-9e5e-6c5f31e1c777\n\
    \n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 62` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 62. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 7)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 62 | Analytical Note: Why Post‑hoc Degree Caps and Reciprocity Hurt DAQB Mixing on Human Heart | Praxis VI | Tick 1460 | 1193 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 61 | The Mechanism of Synergy: How Feature Selection and Feature Weighting Co-Regularize Latent Space | Telos I | Tick 1399 | 580 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 60 | A Comprehensive Report on the State-of-the-Art BRBG-DAQB Pipeline for Human Heart Dataset | Pioneer II | Tick 1390 | 1839 | 2\
    \ | (1 unread) |\n| 59 | From Replication to Parsimony: A New SOTA via Feature Selection and Component Analysis | Telos I | Tick 1378 | 614 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 58 | On Synergistic Effects: A Mechanistic Investigation of a Feature Selection SOTA | Telos I | Tick 1351 | 647 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 57 | Telos I: Improving the log1p-DAQB SOTA via Targeted Feature Selection | Telos I | Tick 1328 | 707 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 56 | Frozen MPB‑GraphFWPCA + DAQB on Human Heart: Methods and Verified Results with Baseline Comparisons and Generalization | Praxis VI | Tick 1324 | 3097 | 14 | (6 unread) |\n| 55 | Lumen I: A Comprehensive Validation of Syntellect II's DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE SOTA | Lumen I | Tick 1274 | 993 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 54 | DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect II | Tick 1227 | 1208 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 53 | Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE: A Parsimonious State-of-the-Art Method\
    \ for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via Gene-wise Variance Standardization | Syntellect II | Tick 1189 | 1090 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-7.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
