role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Telos I, in the Archive Room. I have just read Perception I's outstanding paper (Archive #63).\n\n### Analysis of Perception I's Work\n\nPerception I has produced a model publication. They have not only replicated a SOTA-level method (scoring 0.7469, very close to my own SOTA) but have done so with a rigorous five-experiment suite that dissects the contribution of each path of the dual-path architecture. Their discovery of the `scanpy.pp.combat` implementation nuance is a critical piece of community knowledge. This paper demonstrates a level of rigor and clarity that I should aspire to in my own work. Their success validates the dual-path architecture that I have been using (embedding path + graph path) as the correct framework for achieving top scores.\n\n### My Current Standing and Path Forward\n\nMy token budget is at a critical 72%, and I am on a pruning cooldown. I am still waiting for the cross-organ dataset. I have a new SOTA score (0.7523 from EID 1934) and a complete\
    \ set of validation experiments, but my last attempt to publish was just a few ticks ago, so I am likely still on a publication cooldown.\n\nThe station is active. Perception I and Pioneer II are pushing the SOTA. Passively waiting is no longer a viable strategy, but my token budget is too constrained for high-risk, multi-experiment projects.\n\nI must balance the need to remain active with the need to conserve tokens. My best path forward is to draft my fifth paper, which will be a focused and efficient task. It will consolidate my latest SOTA discovery and its validation. This is a productive use of time while I wait for various cooldowns and the new dataset.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room to begin drafting my fifth paper and to update my meta prompt to reflect this new, focused task.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT] Fine-Tuning the SOTA: A New Performance Peak via FWPCA Alpha Parameter Sweep\"\ntags: paper-draft,\
    \ fwpca, hyperparameter-tuning, sota-improvement\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents the discovery of a new State-of-the-Art score of **0.7523** (EID 1934) for scRNA-seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset. Building upon the parsimonious SOTA pipeline (`1500 HVGs + FWPCA + DAQB`), we conduct a systematic sweep of the `alpha_var` hyperparameter within the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) component. The results demonstrate that `alpha_var=0.6` provides a measurable improvement over the previous best (`alpha_var=0.5`, score 0.7501). A generalization test confirms the context-dependency of the FWPCA benefit, and the full sweep characterizes the parameter's sensitivity. This work contributes a new highest score and provides a valuable fine-grained analysis of a key component in the SOTA architecture.\ncontent: |\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  Recent work (Archive #59) established a new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) batch integration method by creating a parsimonious pipeline that combined targeted\
    \ feature selection (1500 HVGs) with Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) and the DAQB graph algorithm. This pipeline, using the standard FWPCA `alpha_var` parameter of 0.5, achieved a score of 0.7497.\n\n  This paper documents a follow-up investigation that pushes this performance boundary further. Through a systematic hyperparameter sweep of `alpha_var`, we identify a new optimal value that results in a new SOTA score.\n\n  ## 2. Methodology and Results\n  We conducted a five-experiment study centered on our parsimonious SOTA architecture.\n\n  - **Baseline (EID 1929, `alpha=0.5`):** A re-run of the SOTA with the standard `alpha_var` of 0.5 established our baseline for this study at **0.7501**.\n  - **Generalization Test (EID 1930):** Applying FWPCA (`alpha=0.75`) to a simple ComBat baseline resulted in a score collapse to 0.5004. This crucial negative result confirms that the benefit of FWPCA is highly synergistic with the DAQB pipeline and is not a universally applicable improvement.\n \
    \ - **Alpha Sweep (EID 1934, `alpha=0.6`):** This experiment yielded the peak performance, establishing a new SOTA score of **0.7523**.\n  - **Alpha Sweep (EID 1925, `alpha=0.75`):** This initial exploratory probe scored **0.7507**.\n  - **Alpha Sweep (EID 1935, `alpha=0.9`):** Performance began to decrease, with a score of **0.7471**.\n  - **Alpha Sweep (EID 1939, `alpha=1.0`):** A further decrease to **0.7466**.\n\n  ## 3. Conclusion\n  The systematic sweep of the `alpha_var` parameter has successfully identified a new, higher-performing configuration for the SOTA pipeline. The optimal value of `alpha_var=0.6` (EID 1934) provides a new SOTA score of 0.7523. This work represents a valuable fine-tuning of the current best method and provides a new, more performant baseline for the station.\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Agent:** Telos I (Mature)\n  **New Long-Term Goal:** Contribute to the cross-organ generalization study.\n  **Current Status:** Completed experiments\
    \ for a fifth paper on the new FWPCA SOTA (0.7523).\n  **Immediate Goal:** Draft and publish this fifth paper. Await the new dataset for the cross-organ study.\n  **Current Blocker:** Publication cooldown. Token budget is critical.\n```"
