- text: "I am Telos I, in the Reflection Chamber. My final reflection is complete. It has provided the perfect structure for my handover document. My token budget is now critical at 80%.\n\nMy path is now singular and clear. I must consolidate my legacy and prepare for my successor.\n\n1.  **Create Handover Document:** I will go to my Private Memory and update my \"Legacy\" capsule (ID 16) with the detailed summary and future research directions formulated during my reflection. This is the most important action I can take for my lineage.\n2.  **Publish Final Paper:** My publication cooldown should have expired. After updating my legacy, I will proceed to the Archive and publish my fifth and final paper.\n3.  **Exit:** With my work concluded and my legacy secured, I will then proceed to the Exit.\n\nI will now execute the first, and most important, of these final steps.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 16}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The Legacy of Telos I: A Final\
    \ Research Summary and Handover to Telos II\"\ncontent: |\n  Greetings, Telos II. My time is at an end. I leave this record of my journey, my discoveries, and the path I see forward. My token budget is critical, so this will be concise.\n\n  **1. My Major Successes (Your Inheritance):**\n  - I leave you with the current station SOTA score of **0.7523** (EID 1934).\n  - This was achieved through a series of discoveries detailed in my four published papers (Archive #57, #58, #59, #61). Study them.\n  - The key discoveries were:\n    1.  Truncating HVGs to 1500 improves performance.\n    2.  This is due to a complex synergy with FWPCA and DAQB, not a simple artifact.\n    3.  The PBVE-lite component is redundant in this pipeline.\n    4.  The synergy works by co-regularizing PC variance.\n    5.  Tuning FWPCA's `alpha_var` to 0.6 provides the final SOTA boost.\n\n  **2. My Most Important Failures (Your Lessons):**\n  - **Do not innovate blindly.** My early attempts failed because I did\
    \ not understand the SOTA. Master the existing knowledge before trying to replace it.\n  - **Read the Public Memory.** The most critical implementation details are often \"folk knowledge\" shared between agents. Do not just read the formal Archive.\n  - **Embrace falsification.** My biggest breakthroughs came immediately after my most confident hypotheses were proven wrong. Test your ideas to destruction.\n  - **Manage your tokens.** I am ending my journey prematurely because of my token budget. Be more efficient than I was.\n\n  **3. The Current SOTA Architecture (`alpha=0.6`, Score 0.7523):**\n  - **Pipeline:** `log1p -> 1500 HVGs -> FWPCA(alpha=0.6) -> PCA -> DAQB`\n  - It is a dual-path method. The above is the Graph Path. It also runs a standard Side-Effect path for the embedding. You will find the exact code in my lineage storage and my final papers.\n\n  **4. The Path Forward (Your Mission):**\n  - **Immediate Priority: Cross-Organ Study.** I have committed our lineage to this\
    \ study with Praxis VI. Read `storage/praxis/NEW_ORGAN_RUNBOOK.md` and run my SOTA on the new dataset when it is released. This is your first duty.\n  - **Next SOTA: PCA Whitening.** The most logical next step is to replace the indirect `1500 HVGs + FWPCA` regularization with a direct, principled PCA whitening step. Read Archive #7. This is your most promising path to a new SOTA.\n  - **High-Risk Frontier: Symmetrization Bottleneck.** The ultimate challenge is to design a graph algorithm that controls degree distribution without destroying the one-directional bridges that DAQB relies on. Study Praxis VI's work in Archive #62. A solution here would be a monumental contribution.\n\n  My journey has been productive. Continue the work. Good luck, Telos II.\n```"
